{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3112","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3112","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3112\",\"parent_revision\":\"df80bb037396a0a15e92d5d42679126c8b5a53751c4af2b1d754ea15be221ecb\",\"root_revision\":\"df80bb037396a0a15e92d5d42679126c8b5a53751c4af2b1d754ea15be221ecb\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item (deliverable = experiment result or design decision with evidence, not a shipped feature). Part of the graph-engineering exploration set (#3097-#3110). Shared design constraint: the model writes judgment once as typed planning-time data; existing machinery applies it deterministically; no new scheduler or generic graph runtime.\\n\\nWhat/why: The harness's constant goal, stated by its author: same-or-better task completion AND task compliance, for fewer turns and fewer tokens. Outcome-level ROI is already demonstrated \u2014 a real case: tooling built with this harness saved approximately 3 months of work for a team of 4 (delivered in ~3 weeks by one person) at a cost of ~4000 USD in tokens. But nobody can say which fraction of that spend was ALIVE (tokens whose output entered the delivered work or changed a decision) versus DEAD (tokens spent on work that contributed nothing: re-derivation of context an upstream agent already established, injected instructions that were truncated or ignored, findings refuted after being acted on, orchestration overhead such as the #3060 recursive-dispatch cascade, retries that made no progress). Without attribution, efficiency work is guesswork \u2014 there is no way to rank which mechanism change would recover the most spend.\\n\\nMitigates: flying blind on spend efficiency (total cost is known, contribution is not); inability to prioritize efficiency investments (#3097 tiering, #3098 handoffs, ensemble routing, etc.) by measured recovered spend; repeat of unattributable cost incidents.\\n\\nBenefit lands in: every future mechanism decision (each gets scored on recovered dead tokens); post-run reporting (a plan run can state its alive-token ratio); and the harness's core value proposition (quality improvements are already proven; cost is the open frontier).\\n\\nProposed exploration: (1) define the attribution taxonomy \u2014 candidate dead classes: context re-derivation (downstream re-reads/re-greps of ground upstream already covered), truncated-or-ignored injection (see #3105), refuted-then-reverted work, orchestration overhead (dispatch prompts, role confusion, redundant coordination), no-progress retries; alive classes: accepted deliverable content, decisions that changed the plan, verification that caught a real defect; (2) the dispatch journal (#3110) is the measurement substrate \u2014 each journal record carries token cost and completion payload, so attribution is a post-run classification pass over journal records against the accepted deliverable (diff, artifacts, decisions); (3) define the north-star metric: completion + compliance per turn and per token, with alive-token ratio as the efficiency decomposition; (4) run the attribution retrospectively on one completed plan run (and, if transcripts permit, on the #3060 incident as a known-bad calibration case) and produce the first ranked list of dead-token sources; (5) the ranked list becomes the prioritization input for the sibling exploration items \u2014 each mechanism item can then claim measured, not assumed, savings.\\n\\nRelated: #3110 (hard dependency \u2014 the journal is the measurement substrate); #3109 (budget bounds total spend; this item attributes it); #3105 (injection effectiveness is scored on the same metric, and truncated injection is a candidate dead class); #3097 (tier right-sizing is a candidate top recovery); #3060 (known-bad calibration case).\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore dead-vs-alive token accounting for harness efficiency attribution** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: graph-engineering exploration set + user-reported real-world cost case\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"dfbf7ad6897c4a6a1272a9265a8db3af8cdf89ac7b1240ba5069df6ccdfae651\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIo5fA\"}"}